Package: Nestimate
Title: Dynamic, Probabilistic, and Higher-Order Network Analysis
Version: 1.1.1
Authors@R: c(
    person("Mohammed", "Saqr", email = "saqr@saqr.me", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph")),
    person("Sonsoles", "López-Pernas", email = "sonsoles.lopez@uef.fi", role = "aut"),
    person("Kamila", "Misiejuk", email = "kamila.misiejuk@fernuni-hagen.de", role = "aut")
    )
Description: Estimates, compares and tests networks from sequence, event-log,
    panel and cross-sectional data through one interface, build_network().
    Sequence data give transition, frequency, co-occurrence,
    attention-weighted, n-gram, gap and reply networks, following transition
    network analysis Saqr et al. (2025) <doi:10.1145/3706468.3706513>,
    and reply networks weighted by response latency Templeton et al. (2022)
    <doi:10.1073/pnas.2116915119>. Psychological networks are estimated by
    correlation, partial correlation, graphical lasso, Ising and mixed
    graphical models Saqr, Beck, and Lopez-Pernas (2024)
    <doi:10.1007/978-3-031-54464-4_19>, together with multilevel vector
    autoregression Epskamp et al. (2018) <doi:10.1080/00273171.2018.1454823>
    and the network comparison test van Borkulo et al. (2022)
    <doi:10.1037/met0000476>. Higher-order methods cover higher-order
    networks, hyper-path anomalies, multi-order generative models,
    hypergraphs and simplicial complexes. Inference includes bootstrap,
    permutation and Bayesian comparison, split-half reliability and
    centrality stability. Further tools fit mixtures of Markov chains and
    cluster sequences, test Markov order, and compute transition entropy,
    passage times, stability landscapes, sequence patterns and indices, and
    association tests for counts nested in actors.
